WireGuard Client Tunnels Plugin

Creates, binds, monitors and removes WireGuard VPN client tunnels (ProtonVPN
and similar) from one page. The core WireGuard, interface, gateway, route and
NAT pages stay the source of truth: every tunnel is derived from core
configuration on each use, and the plugin stores only which instances it
manages, which gateways it holds down, and its switches.

Features:
- Create a tunnel from a pasted wg-quick config -- WireGuard instance and
  peer, interface, IPv4 and IPv6 gateways, endpoint route to the chosen WAN
  and outbound NAT, in one save -- with the MTU measured from that WAN
- Edit a tunnel in place -- outbound NAT sources, MTU, monitor IP, bound
  WAN, IPv6 on or off, or a server swap from a replacement wg-quick config
  -- with a preview of exactly what changes; its name, device, interface
  and gateway names never change, so groups and rules keep working
- Remove a tunnel and everything it owns (refused while anything references
  it), Rebind an unbound tunnel to a WAN, Adopt an existing WireGuard
  instance, and Ensure sentinel (the NO_DEFAULT4/NO_DEFAULT6 gateways), each
  with a dry-run preview, from the Tunnels tab or tunnel.php
- Tunnels tab: a standard grid of every managed tunnel with its derived
  parts, live gateway status, links to the core page that owns each part,
  and findings; it reloads as soon as an action completes
- Gives each managed tunnel's IPv6 gateway its host route and keeps its
  tunnel address in place: at boot, on gateway and WAN address events, on
  every configuration change, and every minute as a safety net
- Health mirror (every 15 s):
  - binds each tunnel to the WAN its endpoint route uses and holds the
    tunnel down while that WAN is down, so a tunnel never migrates to the
    other WAN
  - forces each IPv6 tunnel gateway down when its own IPv4 tunnel loses
    packets, loses its monitor or is forced down, using that gateway's
    loss thresholds with hysteresis
  - ignores a freshly restarted monitor's readings until they settle, so
    a gateway is never released in the middle of an outage
  - replays the gateway alarm its own reconfigure would otherwise drop,
    so firewall rules and states follow the change
- Default-route guard: removes a default route that points through a
  tunnel or other non-default gateway, for IPv4 and IPv6, working
  together with address-less sentinel gateways that keep tunnels out of
  the default-gateway election
- Firewall enforcement rendered from each tunnel's core configuration:
  - pins each tunnel's endpoint to the WAN it is bound to, and blocks
    it everywhere when that WAN is unusable
  - blocks traffic leaving a tunnel from any source but the firewall
    itself, so only NAT-ed traffic enters it
  - clamps TCP MSS in both directions from each tunnel's MTU, so an
    MTU change on the WireGuard page takes effect without other edits
- Leaves disabled gateways and an operator's manual force-down alone

Plugin Changelog
================

3.1

* Edit a managed tunnel from the Tunnels tab or tunnel.php edit (JSON on
  stdin): outbound NAT sources, MTU, monitor IP, bound WAN, IPv6 on/off and
  a server swap; the preview lists exactly what changes, an edit that
  changes nothing saves nothing, a refusal writes nothing, and the apply
  follows the change (NAT alone reloads the filter without the gateway
  lock; a tunnel change ends with wireguard restart)
* A saved change whose apply did not complete stays apply-pending with the
  apply it still needs; tunnel.php apply UUID (the Apply button) runs that
  apply, and only a complete one clears it. Create's re-run is the tunnel
  apply, which now ends with wireguard restart, so a re-run handshakes via
  the bound WAN; tunnel.php apply UUID filter|routes|first|tunnel names one

3.0

* Renamed from os-wg-ipv6-gateway: package os-wg-client-tunnels, namespace
  OPNsense\WGClientTunnels, configd, pages and API under wgclienttunnels;
  model 3.0.0 copies the 2.x settings and removes the old section (a 1.x
  section must first be migrated by os-wg-ipv6-gateway 2.x)
* The reconcile (every minute and on each config save) migrates an old
  section that config holds without a new one, as a Config History revert
  to a pre-3.0 revision leaves it; a refusal is logged once per state
* Runtime under /var/run/wgclienttunnels and /var/db/wgclienttunnels, pf
  anchor wgclienttunnels_mss, rules described "WireGuard Client Tunnels:",
  syslog tags wgct-*
* tunnels.php is gone; tunnel.php list replaces it
* A WireGuard start, stop, restart or configure step is issued only for an
  instance uuid core recognises; core reads anything else as a CARP vhid
* WireGuard is part of core: the os-wireguard dependency is dropped
* The Tunnels tab lists the managed tunnels in core's grid (sorting,
  search, paging, horizontal scroll), with Remove, Rebind and Apply as row
  commands; the grid, the banners and the unmanaged list reload when an
  action's reply arrives, not only when its result dialog is closed

2.2

* Create a tunnel from a pasted wg-quick config -- WireGuard instance and
  peer, interface, IPv4 and IPv6 gateways, endpoint route and outbound NAT
  -- with the MTU measured from the bound WAN
* Remove, Rebind and Adopt from the Tunnels tab or tunnel.php, each with a
  dry-run preview; Remove refuses while anything references the tunnel
* Ensure sentinel builds the NO_DEFAULT4/NO_DEFAULT6 gateways
* Every action saves once under the config lock, applies through configd
  and replays gateway alarms dropped while it held the gateway lock
* A Create whose apply did not complete shows the apply-pending finding
  and an Apply button, across a reboot
* Create installs the endpoint route before the tunnel's first handshake,
  and Rebind restarts the tunnel, so it handshakes via the bound WAN
* tunnel.php command line; tunnels.php stays as its list alias

2.1

* Rendered WAN pins, inner-source blocks and a TCP MSS clamp (both
  directions) derived from core, each with its own switch
* The configuration reconcile requests a filter reload when the
  rendered rules or the MSS anchor fall out of date, with back-off
* A render-failed finding; the Tunnels tab also shows each tunnel's
  clamp

2.0

* Tunnels are derived from core configuration on every use; the plugin
  keeps only which WireGuard instances it manages (the per-gateway rows
  are migrated away)
* Read-only Tunnels tab with live gateway status, the mirror's held
  marker, links to the owning core pages, and findings
* The health mirror's held set lives in the plugin configuration
* Switches for the health mirror, IPv6 routes and default-route guard
* Reconcile on every configuration change, not only each minute

1.1

* The health mirror re-reads config under the config lock before saving,
  so a change saved in the GUI while it runs is never reverted
* Mirror decisions are a pure function of a snapshot; --snapshot and
  --plan-from=FILE capture and replay one
* Applying the plugin's settings builds its models under the config lock

1.0

* Per-tunnel IPv6 addresses and gateway host routes, restored at boot,
  on gateway and WAN events, and by a one-minute reconcile
* Health mirror with WAN binding, IPv6-follows-IPv4, loss hysteresis and
  a settle guard for freshly restarted monitors
* Serialised reconfigures and a configd alarm replay, so the mirror
  cannot starve itself or drop gateway alarms
* Default-route guard for tunnels and other non-default gateways
* Dashboard service status detection
